WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.690
In 2025, an OpenAI engineer was 8 times more likely to move to Anthropic

2
00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:04.770
than the other way around,

3
00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:06.510
and the reason might not be salary.

4
00:00:06.843 --> 00:00:07.933
For DeepMind, the ratio was almost eleven

5
00:00:07.933 --> 00:00:08.393
to one.

6
00:00:08.393 --> 00:00:09.213
In this video,

7
00:00:09.213 --> 00:00:10.393
I want to show what Anthropic is

8
00:00:10.393 --> 00:00:12.213
doing differently, both with people and with

9
00:00:12.213 --> 00:00:12.813
AI itself.

10
00:00:12.813 --> 00:00:14.413
And why that points to what successful

11
00:00:14.413 --> 00:00:16.553
companies will look like in 2027.

12
00:00:16.553 --> 00:00:19.153
These numbers come from SignalFire’s 2025 talent

13
00:00:19.153 --> 00:00:19.693
report.

14
00:00:19.693 --> 00:00:21.053
They also measured retention:

15
00:00:21.053 --> 00:00:22.033
of the people hired,

16
00:00:22.033 --> 00:00:23.363
how many were still at the company

17
00:00:23.363 --> 00:00:23.873
two years later.

18
00:00:23.873 --> 00:00:24.840
Anthropic,

19
00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:25.213
eighty percent.

20
00:00:25.213 --> 00:00:26.393
DeepMind,

21
00:00:26.393 --> 00:00:26.733
seventy-eight.

22
00:00:26.733 --> 00:00:27.743
OpenAI,

23
00:00:27.743 --> 00:00:28.133
sixty-seven.

24
00:00:28.133 --> 00:00:28.873
Meta, sixty-four.

25
00:00:28.873 --> 00:00:29.793
Now, a word of caution.

26
00:00:29.793 --> 00:00:31.593
This doesn’t mean Anthropic is eight times

27
00:00:31.593 --> 00:00:32.433
better to work for.

28
00:00:32.433 --> 00:00:33.433
It could be rapid growth,

29
00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:34.213
team size, salary,

30
00:00:34.213 --> 00:00:34.913
or equity.

31
00:00:34.913 --> 00:00:36.373
And retention isn’t happiness.

32
00:00:36.373 --> 00:00:37.993
Someone might stay because of the mission,

33
00:00:37.993 --> 00:00:39.553
but also because they can’t find anything

34
00:00:39.553 --> 00:00:40.893
comparable elsewhere.

35
00:00:40.893 --> 00:00:42.213
The report points to other factors,

36
00:00:42.213 --> 00:00:44.053
too: researchers’ autonomy,

37
00:00:44.053 --> 00:00:46.393
flexibility, and less office politics.

38
00:00:46.393 --> 00:00:48.913
One explanation you hear is Maslow’s pyramid.

39
00:00:48.913 --> 00:00:50.413
At the bottom are basic needs:

40
00:00:50.413 --> 00:00:51.933
food and a place to live.

41
00:00:51.933 --> 00:00:53.873
Then safety: a stable job.

42
00:00:53.873 --> 00:00:55.433
Then belonging: being part of a team.

43
00:00:55.433 --> 00:00:57.553
Then esteem: being recognized for what you

44
00:00:57.553 --> 00:00:57.753
do.

45
00:00:57.753 --> 00:00:59.073
And at the top,

46
00:00:59.073 --> 00:01:01.293
self-actualization: using your full potential on something

47
00:01:01.293 --> 00:01:01.833
meaningful.

48
00:01:01.833 --> 00:01:03.433
The idea is that a well-paid,

49
00:01:03.433 --> 00:01:05.973
recognized researcher has the basics covered and

50
00:01:05.973 --> 00:01:07.093
starts looking for meaning.

51
00:01:07.093 --> 00:01:08.693
And there’s a step beyond that,

52
00:01:08.693 --> 00:01:10.353
which some call self-transcendence:

53
00:01:10.353 --> 00:01:13.153
contributing to something bigger than yourself.

54
00:01:13.153 --> 00:01:14.833
Anthropic’s mission speaks to both.

55
00:01:14.833 --> 00:01:16.333
It’s a plausible hypothesis.

56
00:01:16.333 --> 00:01:18.093
But it’s still a hypothesis.

57
00:01:18.093 --> 00:01:19.953
No one surveyed the employees to prove

58
00:01:19.953 --> 00:01:20.113
it.

59
00:01:20.113 --> 00:01:21.833
But I think the most important part

60
00:01:21.833 --> 00:01:22.613
is something else.

61
00:01:22.613 --> 00:01:24.453
In January 2026,

62
00:01:24.453 --> 00:01:26.953
Anthropic published a new constitution for Claude.

63
00:01:26.953 --> 00:01:29.093
Here, a constitution is a text that

64
00:01:29.093 --> 00:01:31.333
explains which values the model should follow,

65
00:01:31.333 --> 00:01:32.193
and why.

66
00:01:32.193 --> 00:01:33.733
And it’s used in training.

67
00:01:33.733 --> 00:01:35.173
It’s not a manual sitting in a

68
00:01:35.173 --> 00:01:35.633
drawer.

69
00:01:35.633 --> 00:01:36.853
There are four priorities,

70
00:01:36.853 --> 00:01:38.153
in this order.

71
00:01:38.153 --> 00:01:40.773
First, be safe and not undermine human

72
00:01:40.773 --> 00:01:41.633
oversight.

73
00:01:41.633 --> 00:01:43.353
Second, be ethical,

74
00:01:43.353 --> 00:01:45.913
honest, and use good judgment.

75
00:01:45.913 --> 00:01:48.933
Third, follow Anthropic’s guidelines.

76
00:01:49.013 --> 00:01:51.273
Fourth, be genuinely helpful.

77
00:01:51.273 --> 00:01:52.953
The big change is moving from rules

78
00:01:52.953 --> 00:01:53.873
to reasons.

79
00:01:53.873 --> 00:01:55.273
Instead of a list of what’s allowed

80
00:01:55.273 --> 00:01:56.073
and what isn’t,

81
00:01:56.073 --> 00:01:57.193
the model learns why.

82
00:01:57.193 --> 00:01:58.773
And that why helps when it faces

83
00:01:58.773 --> 00:02:00.553
a situation no rule anticipated.

84
00:02:00.553 --> 00:02:02.153
Here’s a technical difference that few people

85
00:02:02.153 --> 00:02:02.833
notice.

86
00:02:02.833 --> 00:02:05.073
Anthropic uses the constitution to shape the

87
00:02:05.073 --> 00:02:06.613
model’s behavior during training.

88
00:02:06.613 --> 00:02:08.113
When you build an agent at your

89
00:02:08.113 --> 00:02:09.513
company and give it a document,

90
00:02:09.513 --> 00:02:11.353
that document stays in its context,

91
00:02:11.353 --> 00:02:12.513
in its instructions.

92
00:02:12.513 --> 00:02:13.633
It helps a lot,

93
00:02:13.633 --> 00:02:15.393
but it doesn’t change the model from

94
00:02:15.393 --> 00:02:16.073
the inside.

95
00:02:16.073 --> 00:02:17.133
So in your case,

96
00:02:17.133 --> 00:02:19.413
behavior is shaped by the whole package:

97
00:02:19.413 --> 00:02:21.073
instructions, examples, tests,

98
00:02:21.073 --> 00:02:21.793
and correction.

99
00:02:21.793 --> 00:02:24.073
A polished document alone doesn’t guarantee a

100
00:02:24.073 --> 00:02:24.993
reliable agent.

101
00:02:24.993 --> 00:02:26.753
Something else caught people’s attention:

102
00:02:26.753 --> 00:02:28.413
Anthropic started talking with philosophers,

103
00:02:28.413 --> 00:02:30.193
religious leaders, and ethics experts from different

104
00:02:30.193 --> 00:02:30.813
traditions.

105
00:02:30.813 --> 00:02:31.653
One of its founders,

106
00:02:31.653 --> 00:02:33.173
Chris Olah, even spoke at the Vatican

107
00:02:33.173 --> 00:02:33.613
in May.

108
00:02:33.613 --> 00:02:33.993
Why?

109
00:02:33.993 --> 00:02:35.193
There are two different questions.

110
00:02:35.193 --> 00:02:36.113
The first is practical:

111
00:02:36.113 --> 00:02:37.393
how do you get AI to make

112
00:02:37.393 --> 00:02:38.453
a good choice when a simple rule

113
00:02:38.453 --> 00:02:39.193
doesn’t help?

114
00:02:39.193 --> 00:02:41.033
Human traditions have been thinking about that

115
00:02:41.033 --> 00:02:42.053
for thousands of years.

116
00:02:42.053 --> 00:02:43.273
The second is open-ended:

117
00:02:43.273 --> 00:02:44.833
if AI ever has some kind of

118
00:02:44.833 --> 00:02:46.373
experience, how should we treat it?

119
00:02:46.373 --> 00:02:47.433
No one knows the answer.

120
00:02:47.433 --> 00:02:48.333
And there’s a risk:

121
00:02:48.333 --> 00:02:50.313
confusing an AI that talks beautifully about

122
00:02:50.313 --> 00:02:52.593
morality with one that understands morality the

123
00:02:52.593 --> 00:02:53.473
way we do.

124
00:02:53.473 --> 00:02:54.413
Reflection is welcome,

125
00:02:54.413 --> 00:02:55.833
but it doesn’t replace testing,

126
00:02:55.833 --> 00:02:57.413
evidence, and human oversight.

127
00:02:57.613 --> 00:02:58.453
Put it all together,

128
00:02:58.453 --> 00:03:00.433
and Anthropic is building two cultures at

129
00:03:00.433 --> 00:03:01.093
the same time.

130
00:03:01.093 --> 00:03:01.973
One for people:

131
00:03:01.973 --> 00:03:03.113
a mission, autonomy,

132
00:03:03.113 --> 00:03:04.573
and great people working together.

133
00:03:04.573 --> 00:03:06.233
The other for AI itself:

134
00:03:06.233 --> 00:03:08.893
principles, criteria, and guidance for dealing with

135
00:03:08.893 --> 00:03:09.433
the unknown.

136
00:03:09.433 --> 00:03:10.933
Both aim for the same thing:

137
00:03:10.933 --> 00:03:12.973
someone who exercises judgment instead of just

138
00:03:12.973 --> 00:03:13.473
obeying.

139
00:03:13.473 --> 00:03:15.233
And that’s exactly what companies will need

140
00:03:15.233 --> 00:03:16.153
in 2027.

141
00:03:16.153 --> 00:03:17.573
You can lay the two side by

142
00:03:17.573 --> 00:03:17.973
side.

143
00:03:17.973 --> 00:03:19.273
In people management,

144
00:03:19.273 --> 00:03:20.613
you have a mission and a sense

145
00:03:20.613 --> 00:03:21.173
of purpose.

146
00:03:21.173 --> 00:03:22.053
With an agent,

147
00:03:22.053 --> 00:03:23.813
you have a role and an objective:

148
00:03:23.813 --> 00:03:26.813
“this agent answers customer questions about deliveries.”

149
00:03:26.813 --> 00:03:29.313
Company culture becomes the agent’s constitution and

150
00:03:29.313 --> 00:03:29.933
instructions.

151
00:03:29.933 --> 00:03:31.413
Training becomes examples,

152
00:03:31.413 --> 00:03:32.593
context, and tests:

153
00:03:32.593 --> 00:03:34.513
ten hard cases with the right answers.

154
00:03:34.513 --> 00:03:36.633
Professional autonomy becomes permissions:

155
00:03:36.633 --> 00:03:37.993
the agent can look up an order,

156
00:03:37.993 --> 00:03:39.553
but it can’t issue a refund.

157
00:03:39.553 --> 00:03:41.333
Performance reviews become a scorecard:

158
00:03:41.333 --> 00:03:42.893
a report with scores for things like

159
00:03:42.893 --> 00:03:44.153
accuracy, response time,

160
00:03:44.153 --> 00:03:45.253
and escalated cases.

161
00:03:45.253 --> 00:03:47.853
Ongoing development becomes feedback and agent reviews.

162
00:03:47.853 --> 00:03:50.153
And leadership becomes human oversight and auditing:

163
00:03:50.153 --> 00:03:51.453
someone checks the log of what the

164
00:03:51.453 --> 00:03:52.093
agent did.

165
00:03:52.093 --> 00:03:53.673
But there’s a limit to this comparison.

166
00:03:53.673 --> 00:03:54.773
People have needs,

167
00:03:54.773 --> 00:03:55.953
rights, and lives.

168
00:03:55.953 --> 00:03:58.093
Today, we manage agents based on performance,

169
00:03:58.093 --> 00:03:59.413
reliability, and safety.

170
00:03:59.413 --> 00:04:00.653
We can’t assume they feel what we

171
00:04:00.653 --> 00:04:00.953
feel.

172
00:04:00.953 --> 00:04:02.533
So what will a successful company look

173
00:04:02.533 --> 00:04:03.673
like in 2027?

174
00:04:03.673 --> 00:04:04.693
I see three areas.

175
00:04:04.693 --> 00:04:05.933
People: a clear purpose,

176
00:04:05.933 --> 00:04:08.093
real autonomy, great people working alongside great

177
00:04:08.093 --> 00:04:10.033
people, and consistency between what the company

178
00:04:10.033 --> 00:04:11.073
says and what it does.

179
00:04:11.073 --> 00:04:12.253
And room to disagree,

180
00:04:12.253 --> 00:04:13.833
because a big mission can also create

181
00:04:13.833 --> 00:04:15.173
pressure and conformity.

182
00:04:15.173 --> 00:04:16.293
Processes: mapped out,

183
00:04:16.293 --> 00:04:18.213
with an owner and clear success criteria.

184
00:04:18.213 --> 00:04:19.133
You know what goes in,

185
00:04:19.133 --> 00:04:19.893
what comes out,

186
00:04:19.893 --> 00:04:21.613
and what counts as a good result.

187
00:04:21.613 --> 00:04:22.513
Agents:

188
00:04:22.513 --> 00:04:23.413
with a role,

189
00:04:23.413 --> 00:04:25.733
boundaries, rules for when to ask for

190
00:04:25.733 --> 00:04:27.233
help, and evaluation.

191
00:04:27.233 --> 00:04:29.193
The competition won’t just be about who

192
00:04:29.193 --> 00:04:30.493
has the smartest model.

193
00:04:30.493 --> 00:04:32.553
It’ll be about who best organizes knowledge,

194
00:04:32.553 --> 00:04:34.453
people, processes, agents,

195
00:04:34.453 --> 00:04:35.553
evaluation, and learning.

196
00:04:35.553 --> 00:04:36.953
And that applies to an AI lab

197
00:04:36.953 --> 00:04:37.893
and a small business.

198
00:04:37.893 --> 00:04:40.033
In practice, I wouldn’t copy Claude’s constitution.

199
00:04:40.033 --> 00:04:41.833
I’d make a version for your operation.

200
00:04:41.833 --> 00:04:43.073
One page per agent,

201
00:04:43.073 --> 00:04:44.273
with six things.

202
00:04:44.273 --> 00:04:46.393
Mission: why it exists.

203
00:04:46.393 --> 00:04:48.273
Principles: what matters most.

204
00:04:48.273 --> 00:04:51.013
Priorities: what wins when two things conflict—for

205
00:04:51.013 --> 00:04:53.313
example, safety before speed.

206
00:04:53.313 --> 00:04:55.033
Boundaries: what it never does.

207
00:04:55.033 --> 00:04:56.193
When to escalate:

208
00:04:56.193 --> 00:04:57.693
when it should stop and call a

209
00:04:57.693 --> 00:04:58.053
person.

210
00:04:58.053 --> 00:04:59.753
And examples of hard decisions.

211
00:04:59.753 --> 00:05:01.673
For example, a customer support agent can

212
00:05:01.673 --> 00:05:03.213
change a delivery date on its own.

213
00:05:03.213 --> 00:05:04.973
For refunds up to two hundred dollars,

214
00:05:04.973 --> 00:05:06.553
it prepares the refund for a person

215
00:05:06.553 --> 00:05:07.093
to approve.

216
00:05:07.093 --> 00:05:08.613
If a customer mentions a lawsuit,

217
00:05:08.613 --> 00:05:09.513
it doesn’t respond.

218
00:05:09.513 --> 00:05:10.993
It passes the case straight to a

219
00:05:10.993 --> 00:05:11.373
manager.

220
00:05:11.373 --> 00:05:13.333
And the constitution isn’t written once and

221
00:05:13.333 --> 00:05:13.773
forgotten.

222
00:05:13.773 --> 00:05:15.033
It’s part of a cycle.

223
00:05:15.033 --> 00:05:17.333
Mission, constitution, and context.

224
00:05:17.333 --> 00:05:18.993
The agent carries out tasks with its

225
00:05:18.993 --> 00:05:19.273
tools.

226
00:05:19.273 --> 00:05:21.273
You review the results and evaluate them.

227
00:05:21.273 --> 00:05:23.553
You give feedback and revise the constitution.

228
00:05:23.553 --> 00:05:25.033
Then a new cycle begins,

229
00:05:25.033 --> 00:05:26.273
with better criteria.

230
00:05:26.273 --> 00:05:28.613
The part almost everyone forgets is measurement.

231
00:05:28.613 --> 00:05:30.153
Keep your test cases and a record

232
00:05:30.153 --> 00:05:30.853
of failures.

233
00:05:30.853 --> 00:05:32.753
If the revision didn’t improve the results

234
00:05:32.753 --> 00:05:33.813
on the same tests,

235
00:05:33.813 --> 00:05:35.213
it didn’t improve the agent.

236
00:05:35.213 --> 00:05:37.533
That’s what we call cultivated AI:

237
00:05:37.533 --> 00:05:40.153
shaping behavior over time instead of configuring

238
00:05:40.153 --> 00:05:40.833
it once.

239
00:05:40.833 --> 00:05:42.673
Before, the big challenge was hiring people

240
00:05:42.673 --> 00:05:44.493
with good values and teaching them processes.

241
00:05:44.493 --> 00:05:45.553
Now, on top of that,

242
00:05:45.553 --> 00:05:47.073
we’ll have to build agents that can

243
00:05:47.073 --> 00:05:48.033
understand those values,

244
00:05:48.033 --> 00:05:49.333
carry out those processes,

245
00:05:49.333 --> 00:05:50.893
and recognize when they need to ask

246
00:05:50.893 --> 00:05:51.553
for help.

247
00:05:51.553 --> 00:05:53.293
That’s why management doesn’t matter less in

248
00:05:53.293 --> 00:05:53.933
the age of AI.

249
00:05:53.933 --> 00:05:54.753
It matters more.

250
00:05:54.753 --> 00:05:55.713
Start this week:

251
00:05:55.713 --> 00:05:57.193
choose an agent you use and write

252
00:05:57.193 --> 00:05:58.673
the first page of its constitution.

253
00:05:58.673 --> 00:06:00.193
Its mission, its boundaries,

254
00:06:00.193 --> 00:06:01.453
and when it should call you.

255
00:06:01.453 --> 00:06:02.633
At INEMA dot club,

256
00:06:02.633 --> 00:06:03.933
you’ll find the course that walks you

257
00:06:03.933 --> 00:06:05.073
through it step by step.

258
00:06:05.073 --> 00:06:06.433
It’s open and free.

